Minimal example:
<details>
<summary>Code of the example</summary>

```rust
use bevy::prelude::*;

const USE_ORTHOGRAPHIC: bool = true;

fn main() {
    App::build()
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .add_startup_system(setup.system())
        .run();
}

/// set up a simple 3D scene
fn setup(
    commands: &mut Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    let camera_transform = Transform::from_xyz(0., 0., 4.);
    assert_eq!(camera_transform.forward(), Vec3::unit_z());
    // add entities to the world
    commands
        // cube at 0,0
        .spawn(PbrBundle {
            mesh: meshes.add(Mesh::from(shape::Cube { size: 1.0 })),
            material: materials.add(Color::rgb(0.8, 0.7, 0.6).into()),
            ..Default::default()
        });

    if USE_ORTHOGRAPHIC {
        commands.spawn(OrthographicCameraBundle {
            transform: camera_transform,
            orthographic_projection: bevy::render::camera::OrthographicProjection {
                scaling_mode: bevy::render::camera::ScalingMode::FixedVertical,
                ..Default::default()
            },
            ..OrthographicCameraBundle::new_3d()
        });
    } else {
        commands.spawn(PerspectiveCameraBundle {
            transform: camera_transform,
            ..Default::default()
        });
    }
}

```
</details>

In this example, we create a camera with a transform in positive z, confirm that its forward direction is towards postiive z, and create a cube at the origin. However, the cube is still visible, even though it is behind the camera. This applies for both orthographic and perspective camera bundles

<details>
<summary> Screenshot of perspective bundle </summary>

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/36049421/106359984-0042c580-630e-11eb-9558-d008160bb61e.png)

</details>

This suggests that the camera is looking backwards, which seems peculiar

Revisiting this due to #5670 . I remember that while looking at (ha!) views for shadow mapping, I ended up using glam's look at matrix calculations because bevy's seemed to be the inverse, or vice versa. Anyway, looking at (ha!) the code we have in bevy, this is very confusing:
```rust
    pub fn look_at(&mut self, target: Vec3, up: Vec3) {
        let forward = Vec3::normalize(self.translation - target);
        let right = up.cross(forward).normalize();
        let up = forward.cross(right);
        self.rotation = Quat::from_mat3(&Mat3::from_cols(right, up, forward));
    }
```

Forward in bevy terms is -z. The cross-product in a right-handed coordinate system such as what we use in bevy works like this:
![Right-hand curling fingers around the shortest angle from vector A to vector B with the thumb pointing in the direction perpendicular to the two vectors](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/302146/184522003-41733411-0aff-4838-af6b-7f60984c84d8.png)
(from https://physics.stackexchange.com/a/518085 ). So in the above code, forward is pointing from the target to this transform's translation which is backward, `up.cross(forward)` does indeed point to the right - if you curl the fingers of a right hand from up around to backward and stick the thumb out, it points to the right. And then `forward.cross(right)` is actually backward cross right which does indeed point up.

glam does the following:
```rust
    pub fn look_at_rh(eye: Vec3, center: Vec3, up: Vec3) -> Self {
        glam_assert!(up.is_normalized());
        Self::look_to_lh(eye, eye.sub(center), up)
    }
...
    fn look_to_lh(eye: Vec3, dir: Vec3, up: Vec3) -> Self {
        let f = dir.normalize();
        let s = up.cross(f).normalize();
        let u = f.cross(s);
        Self::from_cols(
            Vec4::new(s.x, u.x, f.x, 0.0),
            Vec4::new(s.y, u.y, f.y, 0.0),
            Vec4::new(s.z, u.z, f.z, 0.0),
            Vec4::new(-s.dot(eye), -u.dot(eye), -f.dot(eye), 1.0),
        )
    }
```
The translation component in the fourth column is also interesting but I'll ignore that for now.

glam would end up calling `look_to_lh` with our equivalent of `self.translation - target`. I think `f`, `s`, and `u` are forward, sideways, and up. They are calculated in the same way as bevy does. However, we use right, up, forward as the _columns_ of the matrix, but glam spreads them across the rows instead. Bevy is using the transpose of the 3x3 compared to glam. The transpose of a pure rotation matrix is its inverse. So that explains me observing that glam and bevy's `look_at` are inverses of each other - @cart .

I don't know why things appear to work properly in bevy given this.
